# Combining 12m, 7m and Total Power data

An interferometer filters out emission on scales larger than its shortest baselines allow. Papers fill that gap
by adding the 7-m array (ACA) in the visibility plane and Total Power (TP) or another single dish, most often by
feathering in the image plane. Two mistakes dominate: assuming the 12m+7m image holds all the flux (it often
holds 20-60%, see Pitfalls), and combining datasets whose weights, flux scales, phase centres or spectral frames
do not match. Every combined product needs a flux-recovery check against single-dish data.

Counts below are from the evidence pack (`skills/packs/array-combination.md`). Where a count is my own tally of
the pack's 240 `array_combination` lines (one per paper), it says so; I left out lines that are off-topic for
this technique (band-to-band images such as 2014MNRAS.444.1821F and 2017A&A...604A.115V, spectral-window
stacking such as 2024A&A...690A..55S, single-array mosaics such as 2014ApJ...784...62A).

## When to use

- The target is extended relative to the 12-m maximum recoverable scale (MRS): molecular clouds, filaments,
  nearby galaxy discs, circumstellar envelopes, the Sun. Authors who skipped short spacings name it as a
  limitation: "Missing flux on large scales due to no Total Power Array" (2016PASJ...68...88K), "Maximum
  recoverable scale ~11" smaller than cavities" (2017MNRAS.468.3266R), "Missing flux on scales >4.6''"
  (2017ApJ...835..174S).
- You need total fluxes, column densities, masses or line ratios over extended regions (e.g. R21 maps from
  12m+7m+TP, 2025ApJ...988..162D; GMC lifecycle maps from 12m+7m+TP, 2019Natur.569..519K).
- The archive holds 12-m, 7-m and TP MOUSs of the same Science Goal, or you are merging compact + extended
  12-m configurations or data from several cycles of the same target.
- You want to add a non-ALMA single dish (IRAM 30m, APEX, Nobeyama 45m, Herschel, SCUBA, BGPS) as short
  spacings. My tally: 23 papers do this.

## When not to use

- **Compact sources well inside the MRS.** 2015A&A...576L...2S argued missing short-spacing flux was negligible
  and used 12-m data only; 2017ApJ...835..108S used only 12-m data because the 7-m total flux differed by <10%.
- **Compact-source extraction or core catalogues.** Papers use the extended data alone so the large-scale
  emission does not bias source finding: 12 m only for core extraction but 12m+7m for lines
  (2018A&A...618L...5N); an extended-only image for compact sources (2018ApJ...855...24P); baselines >250 m only
  for one analysis (2025ApJ...992..103Z); TM1 left out of line cubes (2021A&A...656A..60A). Use the
  `core-extraction-cmf` or `source-extraction-counts` skill.
- **Fitting models to visibilities.** Fit the 12-m and ACA visibilities jointly instead of combining images
  (2021AJ....161..271F with RADMC-3D and emcee; 2019A&A...628A.100D with galario and dynesty; 2018A&A...617A..23B
  with uvmultifit on main array + ACA only). Use `uv-plane-fitting`.
- **Combining bands** (Band 3 + Band 7, 2017A&A...604A.115V) is a spectral-index problem, not short-spacing
  correction. Use `spectral-index`.
- **Solar data** follow their own TP calibration and feathering chain (2020A&A...635A..71W with SoAP;
  2017ApJ...845L..19B). Treat solar TP absolute temperature as a separate calibration problem.

## Steps

1. **Inventory and decide.** Goal: know what arrays exist and what each will be used for.
   - Read the MOUS list (12-m TM1/TM2, 7-m, TP) and the 12-m MRS. Decide which products you need. Several
     papers make more than one: TP+7m and TP+7m+12m image sets (2020ApJ...894...87C); 7m+TP for one criterion,
     12m+7m+TP for others (2021A&A...656A.133Q); ACA-only cubes at 15" next to 12m+7m cubes (2021A&A...656A..46M).
2. **Calibrate each dataset separately, then check flux scales.** Goal: datasets agree before merging.
   - "calibrated separately, then combined" is the normal order (2014A&A...570L..14R; 2019ApJ...879...25K;
     2020A&A...633A..13D). Re-run the pipeline with QA flag files and flux.csv when needed (2018MNRAS.480.4974H).
   - Compare fluxes on overlapping uv scales: 12-m vs ACA within 10% (2020ApJ...889....9M) or 15%
     (2018ApJ...863..143C); check flux consistency of configurations before combining (2017ApJ...851...83C).
3. **Align phase centres, positions and spectral frames.** Goal: identical sky and frequency grids.
   - Fix positional offsets between short- and long-baseline data (2020ApJ...889L..24P; 20 and 40 mas offsets in
     2019AJ....158...15P). Correct proper motion (2019A&A...625A.118K; 2016ApJ...820L..40A) or assign all epochs
     the first-epoch position (2017ApJ...850L...6A).
   - CASA tasks seen: `fixvis` (2018ApJ...868L...5K), `fixvis` + `fixplanets` (2019ApJ...883...98Z),
     `phaseshift`/`fixplanets` (2025ApJ...990..183U), `fixplanets` + `cvel` for a moving target
     (2016ApJ...821L...8S).
   - Convert to a common frame (LSRK) before combining (2016A&A...587A..44P; 2016A&A...594A..81P) and regrid
     channels (`mstransform` to 0.25 km/s, 2018ApJ...852..122H; `cvel`, 2025SciA...11.4512O).
   - Continuum-subtract each dataset before concatenating, as in 2020ApJ...889....9M and 2018A&A...616A..34H.
4. **Self-calibrate in the right order (12-m multi-configuration).** Goal: consistent gains across configurations.
   - Most common pattern: self-calibrate the short-baseline data, concatenate with long-baseline data, then
     self-calibrate jointly (2018ApJ...869L..41A; 2025ApJ...989....3D; 2025ApJ...993..120H; also joint
     self-cal of C43-8 + C43-5 in 2025ApJ...986L..11S). Some self-calibrate each configuration first
     (2019ApJ...873...73Z).
5. **Set relative visibility weights.** Goal: each array contributes according to its real sensitivity.
   - Check the weights before trusting `concat`. Papers recomputed them with `statwt` (2016ApJ...832..158Z;
     2017A&A...600A.141K; 2017ApJ...835..174S; 2017ApJ...850...83F), used empirical noise-based weights
     (2019Natur.569..519K), scaled ACA down by 0.3 (2014A&A...570L..14R) or set the ACA/12-m ratio to 0.18
     (2020A&A...633A..13D), weighted ACA:TM1:TM2 by intrinsic sensitivity, about 1:5:6 (2018A&A...616A..34H), or
     used `visweightscale` in `concat` (2019ApJ...870...39V). One paper checked that average weights were
     similar and kept defaults (2020A&A...634L..12D).
   - General practice: inspect weights per array with `plotms` (weight vs uv distance) before and after.
6. **Combine 12-m and 7-m interferometric data.** Goal: one deconvolution over all baselines.
   - The dominant method is visibility-domain: `concat` the MSs (2015Natur.527...70P; 2021ApJ...908..231M) or pass
     both to one `tclean` call (2018ApJ...853..160C; 2020MNRAS.497.3504T; joint imaging in 2015ApJ...804...37L).
     Use `copypointing=False` in `concat` when not mosaicking (2018MNRAS.481.4931E).
   - Less common: clean 12-m and 7-m separately and feather the images (2019ApJ...871..170M;
     2016ApJ...826...26T), or use the ACA-only image as `startmodel`/`modelimage` for the 12-m clean, then feather
     (2018ApJ...863..143C; 2020A&A...635A...2S).
7. **Add Total Power or another single dish.** Goal: restore the zero and shortest spacings.
   - **Feather (image plane), most common.** My tally: of 40 papers that combine ALMA TP, 23 state feathering
     (e.g. 2017ApJ...841..110S; 2018ApJ...862....8T; 2020ApJ...904L..24S; 2020ApJ...902...53S), 1 combines in
     Fourier space (2019Natur.569..519K), 4 combine in the uv domain, and 12 cards do not state the method.
     Recipe seen: regrid the single-dish cube, multiply it by the interferometer (mosaic) primary-beam response,
     then feather (2019A&A...623A..96Y). In CASA: `imregrid` the TP to the interferometer grid, `immath` with the
     `.pb` image, `feather(imagename, highres, lowres)`, then primary-beam correct. The PHANGS-ALMA pipeline does
     regrid, continuum subtraction, `tclean`, pb correction, feather with TP and convolution to a round beam
     (2025ApJ...988..162D).
   - **Single dish as a clean model.** TP regridded and used as the clean model, then feather
     (2017A&A...605A..28D); 12m+7m cleaned with the TP image as model, positive clean components smoothed with
     `imsmooth` and feathered with TP, then cleaned again (2018MNRAS.475..448T); Model-Assisted Cleaning plus
     Feather (MACF) with IRAM 30m (2024A&A...690A.375S).
   - **Joint deconvolution in the uv domain.** TP2VIS with TP weight set from the TP map rms (2018ApJ...867..166S);
     `sdintimaging` with APEX (2026ApJ..1003...68Y); almica for 12m+ACA+TP (2025ApJ...992L..15L); concatenation
     with TP converted at 41 Jy/K (2019ApJ...887...88E); single-dish pseudo-visibilities in GILDAS/MAPPING, TP to
     ACA over 0-5 m and then to 12 m over 0-12 m (2025A&A...696A.100G), or IRAM 30m to 7m over 0-7 m after
     multiplying by the 7m primary beam (2026A&A...707A.120H); MIRIAD with Nobeyama 45m pseudo-visibilities and
     a tuned relative weight (2018PASJ...70...73H); NRO45 map converted to uv data (2018ApJ...854...90E).
8. **Image and restore.** Goal: a deconvolved cube with a known beam and flux scale.
   - Typical settings are in the parameter table. Report beam, rms, and which arrays went into each product.
9. **Verify flux recovery.** See Verification checks. Do this before any mass or column density.

